(gemini-cli-extensions/workspace, Apache-2.0), vendored under src/vendored/ (services + utils).

`scripts/sync-upstream.mjs` pulls a newer upstream revision over the vendored tree. It READS THE TWO
LISTS BELOW to decide what it may overwrite, so the lists are the contract, not documentation of it:
edit them in the same commit that forks or unforks a file. The script refuses to run if either list
is unparseable or names a file that is not on disk -- an empty list would otherwise read as
"nothing is protected" and overwrite every fork in silence.

## Files intentionally forked from upstream

These no longer match gemini-cli-extensions/workspace byte-for-byte. `sync-upstream.mjs` reports them
as CONFLICT and leaves them alone; whoever syncs merges them by hand:

- src/vendored/services/DocsService.ts — BOUNDS: `getText` caps the returned text
  (20,000 characters by default, 1–100,000 via `limit`, 2,000 via `preview`) and ends a cut body with the
  shared truncation marker that names its true size.
- src/vendored/services/DriveService.ts — SECURITY: Prevent path traversal in relative localPath; BOUNDS:
  `getComments` takes `pageSize`/`pageToken` and returns `nextPageToken` instead of a bare array that
  dropped the cursor.
- src/vendored/services/CalendarService.ts — PERFORMANCE: Using Date.parse() instead of new Date().getTime() skips object allocation and reduces GC pressure; BOUNDS: `listEvents` takes `maxResults`/`pageToken` and returns `{events, nextPageToken}` instead of a bare array that dropped the cursor.
- src/vendored/utils/MimeHelper.ts — the multipart boundary suffix could be empty
  (`.substring(7)` on a short `Math.random().toString(36)`, e.g. 0.5 -> "0.i"), so two messages
  built in the same millisecond collided on their boundary. Now a randomUUID-derived suffix.
- src/vendored/services/CalendarValidation.ts — optimization: Date.parse vs new Date().getTime()
- src/vendored/services/SheetsService.ts — getText read N tabs in N `values.get` calls; now one
  `values.batchGet` with a per-range fallback that preserves partial success. Sheet names are
  also quote-escaped so a tab named `It's here` yields a parseable A1 range.
- src/vendored/utils/MimeHelper.ts — the multipart boundary suffix could be empty
  (`.substring(7)` on a short `Math.random().toString(36)`, e.g. 0.5 -> "0.i"), so two messages
  built in the same millisecond collided on their boundary. Now a randomUUID-derived suffix.
- src/vendored/utils/validation.ts — optimization: Date.parse vs new Date().getTime()
- src/vendored/services/ChatService.ts — BOUNDS: `listSpaces` takes `pageSize`/`pageToken` and returns
  `{spaces, nextPageToken}` instead of a bare array that dropped the cursor.

## Files that replace an upstream file (ours, never synced)

Upstream has files at these paths too, but ours share nothing with them beyond the name: they are
shims that decouple the vendored services from gemini-cli's own infrastructure. Overwriting one with
its upstream namesake does not produce a merge conflict, it produces a build that pulls gemini-cli
back in -- so `sync-upstream.mjs` never copies over them and never reports them:

- src/vendored/utils/paths.ts — this server's own config/cache locations, not gemini-cli's.
- src/vendored/utils/logger.ts — logs to stderr, since stdout is the MCP transport. Line prefix
  is `[workspace]`, renamed together with the repo (drift: ours, not an upstream change).
- src/vendored/auth/AuthManager.ts — reads `currentAccount()` and delegates to `WorkspaceAuth`,
  where upstream reads gemini-cli's credential files.

Tests covering the forked behaviour live next to each file (`*.test.ts`); they are ours, not
upstream's, and `src/vendored/**` stays excluded from the coverage gate.
